// A function may not be named after a runtime helper.
//
// Every id function is emitted as `id_<name>`, and the runtime prelude already
// defines a C function for each name in resv_names() -- so an id function
// called `str_of_int` becomes `id_str_of_int`, which the prelude declares.
//
// Left unchecked this was a C error attributed to nobody: `cc` reported
// "conflicting types for 'id_str_of_int'", idc.py passed that through as a
// bare "C compilation failed", and bin/idc -- whose emitted C had genuinely
// failed to compile -- reported it as "this is a bug in the self-hosted
// compiler; please report it", inviting a user to file a bug about their own
// typo.
//
// It sits with the duplicate-NAME check because it is the same kind of rule:
// both say a name is already taken, and both are decided from the function
// name alone. resv_names_src (compiler/parse/conf.id) is hand-maintained in
// step with the runtime prelude (back/tgt/c/runtime/prelude.id); tests/stdlib.sh
// checks the two have not drifted. init_resv_names splits it into resv_names,
// the way init_bnames splits builtin_src (exports/extern/builtin/more/names.id).

// Not asked of the runtime itself: `--runtime` says this project IS the
// implementation of those helpers, so a function named `str_of_int` here is
// the definition of `id_str_of_int` rather than a collision with one.
// This used to be its own function, resv_at, called exactly once as the last
// action of dupfn_at (../dup/dup_name.id); the check now inlines there.

resv_err(int i) {
  string prog_loc_v = prog_loc(i);
  string s1_of_v = (import ns1)[((import prog)[i])];
  print(prog_loc_v + "function '" + s1_of_v + "' collides with a runtime helper: every id function is emitted as 'id_<name>', and the runtime already defines 'id_" + s1_of_v + "'. Choose another name");
} chain (int i) {
  note_failure();
} return void;
